## A B S T R A C T
Assembling cellulose nanocrystals (CNCs) can induce photonic emission. It is free of photobleaching and aggregation-induced quenching, which has great potential in the application of information security. However, the emission suffers a low emission quantum yield (EQY), and the assembly mediate (solvents) can affect the assembly or the emission. Herein, we established a strategy of nanoscale assembly to integrate emission enhancement methods and improve the assembly induced emission. Via controlling the CNC assembly in the nanoscale space of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), the EQY of CNC array primarily increased by $50\%$ . When carbon nanodots were further incorporated in MOF, their plasmonic resonance could enhance the emission coupling rate and offset the loss in emission intensity from the extinction of MOF particles, leading to a 6.9-time increase in EQY (to $64.84~\%$ ). Such a high EQY from two emission enhancement mechanisms make this nano­ material able to hide and present photonic information effectively.
## 1. Introduction
Cellulose nanocrystals (CNCs) can induce structural colors through self-assembly, which belongs to physical colors that generated from the light interference, scattering, or diffraction in micro/nanostructure (Kim, Lee, Lee, & Kim, 2019; Meng et al., 2019; Chen et al., 2019; Liu et al., 2019; Hail, Schnoering, Damak, Poulikakos, & Eghlidi, 2020; Sun et al., 2020). The achiral assembly of CNCs was recently reported to be able to present a noniridescent structural color and further cause blue emission without any fluorophores (Gan et al., 2019). That emission was caused by electron transition from virtual states to the base state, making the emission free of photobleaching (Nie & Emery, 1997; Choi, Kim, Oh, & Nam, 2019; Prinz, Matkovic, Pesic, Gajic, & Bald, 2016; Hennig, Monkemoller, Boger, Muller, & Huser, 2015). However, the emission quantum yield (EQY) based on this transition is rather low (Chen, Han, & Wu, 1993; Gan et al., 2015), compared to the solid-state
fluorescent materials.
Fortunately, many works have been reported to enhance photo­ luminescence and their optical signals, especially based on plasmonic resonance (Wu, Zhu, Dufresne, & Lin, 2019, 2019b). For example, car­ bon nanodots (CNDs) were reported to be used as surface plasmon to enhance the quantum efficiency of quantum dots (Junka, Guo, Filppo­ nen, Laine, & Rojas, 2014; Guo, Shan, Liu, Lou, & Shen, 2015). However, this method was seldom applied in the solid-state emission, because the surface plasmons could not have a direct physical interaction with the luminous substrate (Chou et al., 2016; Zhang et al., 2012).
Another kind of technology to enhance the emission quantum effi­ ciency based on the assembly-induced emission of CNCs is to control the assembly of CNCs, such as layer-by-layer assembly, evaporation-induced assembly, coassembly with anionic or neutral polymers, and so on (Podsiadlo et al., 2005; Majoinen, Kontturi, Ikkala, & Gray, 2012; Dumanli et al., 2014; Bardet, Belgacem, & Bras, 2015). Unfortunately, those works mainly focused on a chiral assembling structure, which results in iridescence and lead to reading information wrongly. The chiral structure comes from the electric repulsion between CNC parti­ cles, which usually adsorb intensive charges due to their high aspect ratio (Xiang et al., 2019; Li, Wang, Long, Gan, & Huang, 2019). Although the chirality could be weakened by introducing external force fields of gravity or capillary into assembly process, the force fields loosened the CNC arrays and made them brittle. Moreover, the chirality could not be removed completely, and the EQY was still low (Diaz, Wu, Martini, Youngblood, & Moon, 2013; Gan et al., 2019). Thus, the control on the CNC assembling process should be focused on assembling CNC closely in a nanoscale environment.
Herein, we established a new strategy to control the assembly structure of CNC in the nanoscale space of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and integrate emission-enhancement technologies. Via quanti­ tatively adjusting the chemical surface structure of CNC, the particle number of CNC assembled in the MOF of zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) decreased to a single-digit level. Both the emission enhancement from the nanoscale assembly and decrease from the high refractive index of ZIF-8 were studied. Carbon nanodots, acting as conductive nanoparticles and inducing plasmonic resonance enhance­ ment, were then introduced into MOF to remove that decrease effect as shown in Scheme 1. The EQY was further controlled by adjusting the plasmonic resonance wavelength of CND, which should be close to the maximum excitation wavelength and emission wavelength of the assembly-induced emission of CNC. It is hypothesized that such a strategy can help design new assembly structures of cellulose-based nanomaterials with an improved EQY, and the carbohydrate material should have great potential in hiding optical information.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Experimental materials and instruments
Cotton linter was supplied by Hubei Chemical Fiber Group Co., Ltd. (China). Zinc nitrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , AR), citric acid monohydrate $(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{8}0_{7}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , AR) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH, GR) were purchased from Chengdu Keshi Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (China). Sodium hy­ droxide (NaOH, $96.0~\%$ , granular) and methyl alcohol $\mathrm{\CH_{3}O H}$ , AR) Chongqing Chuandong Chemical Industry (Group) Co., Ltd. 2-methyli­ midazole $\mathrm{(C_{4}H_{6}N_{2},}$ , $98\ \%$ ) was purchased from Aladdin Chemistry. Urea was purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. (China).
## 2.2. Preparation of CNC
The Cellulose nanocrystal (CNC) was extracted by a $\mathrm{H}_{2}S{\cal O}_{4}$ -hydro­ lysis method. Firstly, $25g$ cotton linters were immersed into a $1\mathrm{L}2\mathrm{wt}\%$ aqueous NaOH solution for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ under stirring at room temperature. The solution was washed to neutral, and stirred at $45^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $60\mathrm{min}$ in a 250 mL $65~\%\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to remove the amorphous region from cellulose fibers. The obtained suspension was cooled down to room temperature and centrifuged (4000 rpm, 5 min) for at least three times with distilled water to neutral. To obtain solid-state CNCs, the suspension needed to be dialyzed for $^{5\mathrm{d}}$ and lyophilized.
## 2.3. Preparation of ZIF-8
$0.893\mathrm{~g~}$ $\cdot3.00\ \mathrm{mmol}_{.}^{\cdot}$ $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ and $_{0.985\mathrm{~g~}}$ ( $\left\mathrm{12.0~mmol}\right\rangle$ 2- methylimidazole were immersed into $10.0\ \mathrm{mL}$ and $15.0\ \mathrm{mL}$ methyl alcohol respectively. After being solved completely, 2-methylimidazole was added into $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ slowly, and the mixture was stirred at 45 $\square600\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Before washing the sediment with methyl alcohol, the obtained suspension was placed for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to steady the crystals. After centrifuging (10,000 rpm, 5 min) for three times, the obtained ZIF-8 was placed into vacuum drying chamber at $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .
## 2.4. Preparation of ZIF-8@CNC
