$0.10g$ ZIF- $8@\mathsf{C N C}$ was immersed into $10.0~\mathrm{mL}$ above CND solution $(0.15~\mathrm{\wt\%})$ , stirring for $^{15\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $45\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Then, the suspension was centrifuged (10,000 rpm, $5\mathrm{min}\dot{}$ ) for three times and dried in vacuum drying chamber at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.7. Characterizations
2.7.1. Morphology of CNC, CND, ZIF-8@CNC and CND@ZIF-8@CNC
2.7.1.1. Atomic force microscope (AFM). The morphology of CNC and ZIF-8 coated CNC (ZIF-8@CNC) were observed by Atomic Force Mi­ croscope (Icon dimension; Bruker, Germany) in Scanasyst-Air mode. The appropriate concentration of CNC and ZIF- $8@\mathsf{C N C}$ suspension were dropped on the Highly-Oriented Pyrolytic Graphite substrates and dried for measurement. The dimensions of samples were estimated from 100 samples by the associated software.
2.7.1.2. Transmission electron microscope (TEM). TEM was used to observe the morphology of Carbon nanodots (CND) and CND decorated ZIF-8@CNC $\mathrm{\underline{{{CND}}}}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8@\mathrm{CNC})$ by Talos F200X (Thermo Fisher Sci­ entific, Czech). The appropriate concentration of CND and $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF}.$ $8@\mathsf{C N C}$ suspension were dropped on the Carbon support film and dried for measurement.
## 2.7.2. Structure property characterization
2.7.2.1. X-ray diffraction (XRD). XRD was used to study the structure property of ZIF- ${\cal8}@{\cal C}{\mathrm{NC}},$ , and it was carried out on X-Ray diffractometer (XRD-6000; Shimadzu, Japan). Cu Kα radiation was operated at $\lambda=$ $0.154~\mathrm{nm}$ from $3^{\circ}$ to $40^{\circ}$ with a step speed of $2^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ .
2.7.2.2. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR). The Fourier infrared spectroscopy analysis of CNC, ZIF-8 and ZIF-8@CNC was con­ ducted on Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Madison, WI 53711, USA, $4000{-}400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ).
2.7.2.3. X-Ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The X-Ray photoelec­ tron analysis was conducted on a VG Multilab 2000X (Thermal Electron, USA) to study the change of binding energy between CNC, ZIF-8 and ZIF${8@\mathrm{CNC}}$ .
2.7.2.4. Chemical components analysis. The contents of ZIF- $\scriptstyle\cdot8@\mathbf{CNC}$ and CNC was studied by Chemical Components Analysis (ELEMENTAR VARIO MICRO SELECT, Elementar Analysensysteme, Germany).
## 2.7.3. Photoluminescence property
2.7.3.1. Photoluminescence measurement. The photoluminescence in­ tensity of ZIF- $8@\mathrm{CNC}$ , CNC, ZIF-8, CND, CND@ZIF-8, CND@CNC and $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ was conducted on a 5JI-004 Photoluminescence spectrophotometer (Hitachi High-technologies Corporation, Japan) in Luminescence mode. All the samples were tested in powder form. The scan speed of each sample was $240\mathrm{nm/min}$ , and the excitation slit and emission slit are $2.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ .
2.7.3.2. Photoluminescence emission quantum yield measurement. The emission quantum yield of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ was studied by a FLS980 Spectrometer (Edinburgh Instruments, UK). A blank sample (Empty quartz cell) was scanned to obtain the photoluminescence spectra for reference, and then $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ was sealed in the sample cell and scanned. The data was analyzed by the software of FLS980. All the samples were tested in powder form and the emission quantum yield (EQY) can be calculated by Eq. (1):
$$
E O Y=B_{2}/(A_{1}-B_{1})
$$
where $A_{1}$ is the integrals of the excitation region of blank sample, $A_{2}$ is the integrals of the emission region of blank sample, $B_{1}$ is the integrals of the excitation region of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ , and $B_{2}$ is the integrals of the emission region of CND@ZIF-8@CNC.
## 3. Result and discussion
## 3.1. Assemble cellulose nanocrystals in the nanoscale space of metalorganic frameworks
The diameter/length of CNC increased from 0.034 to 0.07 after CNC reacted with ZIF-8 $(\mathsf{Z I F-}8@\mathsf{C N C})$ , as shown in Fig. 1, which suggested that CNC was assembled in the nano-environment of ZIF-8. The as­ sembly should be ascribed to the change in surface charge, as shown in Fig. 2. The surface charge of CNC decreased from $-38.7\mathrm{eV}$ to $-11.0\mathrm{eV}$ after it reacted with ZIF-8, weakening the electrostatic repulsion be­ tween CNC particles and promoting their assembly. The X-ray diffrac­ tion (XRD) also showed that the ZIF- $8@\mathsf{C N C}$ presented strong diffractions at $7.4^{\circ}$ , $10.4^{\circ}$ , $12.7^{\circ}$ , and $18.0^{\circ}$ , should be attributed to the (110), (200), (211), and (222) facets of ZIF-8 (Fig. 3a), respectively (Guan et al., 2017). Those facets came from the face-centered-cube crystal structure of ZIF-8, which was consistent with change in the cross profile of CNC particles from rod-like to cuboid-like (Fig. S1). Those results indicated that CNCs were successfully introduced into the nanoscale environment of ZIF-8.
The interaction between ZIF-8 and CNCs in the ZIF- $8@\mathrm{CNC}$ was studied by Fourier transformed infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The FT-IR spectrum of ZIF-8 showed a sharp peak of -C-N bonds at $1144~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , which shifted to a highfrequency of $1164~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in ZIF- $\mathsf{8@C N C}$ (Fig. 3b). The broad adsorp­ tion of CNC at $3344~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which was attributed to the stretching vi­ brations of hydroxyl groups, showed a high-frequency shift to 3410 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ in ZIF- $8@\mathsf{C N C}$ (Fig. 3b), indicating that the H-bonds between hydroxyl groups of CNC were weakened. This feature suggested that the -C-N bond in ZIF-8 formed H-bonds with the hydroxyl group of CNC (Zhu et al., 2018). Furthermore, the XPS peaks of oxygen in the hydroxyl groups of CNC at $531.3\mathrm{eV}$ shifted to a higher energy level of $533.1\mathrm{eV}$ in ZIF-8@CNC.The peaks at $399.0\mathrm{eV}$ and $400.5\mathrm{eV}.$ , which ascribed to the -C-N-C bond and the $-N-C=N$ bond in the imidazole group of ZIF-8, also shifted to a higher energy level of $399.6\mathrm{~eV}$ and $400.9\mathrm{~eV}$ respectively (Fig. 3c and 3d) (Wei, Li, & Zhang, 2017). Such three shifts confirmed the H-bonds between ZIF-8 and CNC.
To study the assembly structure of CNC in the nanoscale environ­ ment of ZIF-8 quantitatively, the following equations were used to calculate the average number of CNCs assembling in the ZIF-8@CNC.
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{m}=\uprho\mathrm{V}}\\ &{\mathrm{m}_{\mathrm{t}}=\uprho_{\mathrm{z}}\mathrm{Vx}+\uprho_{\mathrm{c}}\mathrm{V}(1-\mathrm{x})}\\ &{\mathrm{V}=\mathrm{L}\big(0.25\uppi\mathrm{D}^{2}\big)}\end{array}
$$
where $m_{\mathrm{t}}$ is the mass of ZIF- $8@\mathrm{CNC}$ , $\rho_{\mathrm{z}}$ is the density of ZIF-8, $\rho_{\mathrm{c}}$ is the density of CNC, $V$ is the volume of ZIF- ${8@\mathrm{CNC}}$ , $x$ is the volume ratio of ZIF-8@CNC, $L$ is the length of $\mathsf{Z I F-}8@{\mathsf{C N C}},$ and $D$ is diameter of ZIF${8@\mathrm{CNC}}$ . The density of CNC was $1.56\times10^{6}~\mathrm{g}.\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ , and the density of ZIF-8 was $9.5\times10^{5}{{\bf\mathrm{g}}}{{\cdot}}{{\bf{m}}^{-3}}$ (Park et al., 2006; Paralikara, Simonsen, & Lombardi, 2008). Since
$$
m_{\mathrm{z}}=\rho_{\mathrm{z}}V x
$$
where $m_{\mathrm{z}}$ is the mass of ZIF-8, we then obtained the mass ratio of ZIF-8 in ZIF-8@CNC $(\omega_{\mathrm{ZIF}-8})$ as Eq. (6):
