$$
\omega_{\mathrm{{ZIF}-8}}=\frac{m_{\mathrm{{z}}}}{m_{\mathrm{{t}}}}=\frac{\rho_{\mathrm{{z}}}V x}{\rho_{\mathrm{{z}}}V x+\rho_{\mathrm{{c}}}V(1-x)}
$$
where the nitrogen content of $\mathsf{Z I F{-}}8@\mathsf{C N C}.$ , obtained from elemental analysis, was $1.04\%$ , and the theorical nitrogen content of ZIF-8 was 25 $\%$ , so the value of ωZIF- $^{\cdot8}$ should be 0.0416 and the value of $x$ was 0.066.
According to the data collected from atomic force microscopy (AFM) images, we found that the average volume of ZIF- $\cdot8@\mathrm{CNC}$ , which the ωZIF-8 was 0.0416, was $1.03\times{10}^{-\bar{2}3}\mathrm{m}^{3}$ . Then, the volume of CNC in ZIF$8@{\mathsf{C N C}}$ could be calculated by Eq. $(7)$ :
$$
V_{c}=V(1-x)
$$
since the volume of CNC was about $2.24\times10^{-24}\mathrm{m}^{3}$ (from AFM image of Fig. 2a), a ZIF- $\mathsf{8@C N C}$ particle should contain about 4 CNC particles.
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images of ZIF- $8@\mathsf{C N C}$ proved the number of CNC in ZIF-8 (Fig. S2 and S3). However, the low conductivity of ZIF- $\operatorname{\cdot}8@\operatorname{CNC}$ made the particles easily melt under the electron beam, and only ZIF-8 (when CNCs could not) could maintained their morphology.
Fortunately, two facts could prove the increase in achiral degree of CNC assembly that dominated the intensity of assembly-induced emis­ sion. Firstly, the decrease in surface charge should lead to a decrease in chiral degree of CNC assembly (Beck-Candanedo, Roman, & Gray, 2005; Shafiei-Sabet, Hamad, & Hatzikiriakos, 2013; Honorato-Rios et al.,
2016). Secondly, the length/diameter ratio $\left(L/D\right)$ of ZIF@CNC was 16.7, which meant the minimal orientation degree $\zeta O D_{\mathrm{min}},$ proportional to achiral degree) should be 0.999, which was calculated by Eq. (8):
$$
\mathrm{ODmin~}=\frac{\frac{2}{3}\left\{1+\cos^{2}[\arctan(\sqrt{n}L/D)]\right\}-1}{2}
$$
Considering the orientation degree of CNC assembly without ZIF-8 should be 0.993 (Equation S1) (Gan et al., 2019), we believed that achiral degree of CNC was improved in the nano-environment of ZIF-8.
## 3.2. Enhance the photonic emission of CNC assembly
As shown in Fig. 4, after assembling in ZIF-8, the photoluminescence (PL) intensity of CNC increased by about $50~\%$ , compared to CNCs assembling via solvents vaporing (Fig. 4a and 4c). To study the mech­ anism of the photoluminescence phenomenon, we tested the photo­ luminescence (PL) intensity of ZIF-8 and carbon nanodots (CNDs) in solid state. Since the CND may suffer the aggregation induced quench­ ing, we diffused CNDs into ZIF-8 pores. As shown in Fig. 4a and 4b, both ZIF-8 and the CND presented weak PL intensity in solid state, and the maximum excitation wavelength and maximum emission wavelength of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ were equal to those of unmodified CNC. This result indicated that this increase must come from the nanoscale assembly structure of CNC.
Via further modifying CNDs into the ZIF- $\scriptstyle\cdot8@\mathrm{CNC}$ system (Fig. S4), the intensity of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ enhanced remarkably, and the emission quantum yield (EQY) of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ reached $64.84~\%$ (from $9.37\%$ of CNC), much higher than that of the CNC arrays directly from evaporation-induced assembly $(13.4\%)$ (Gan et al., 2019). Such a 6.9-time increase in EQY made the $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ suspension capable for invisible inks, as shown in Fig. 5. Notably, the photo­ luminescent intensity of inks with CNC only was not strong enough for the hidden information to be easily read under ultraviolet light (Fig. S5).
## 3.3. The mechanism of photoluminescence enhancement
The increase in EQY should come from the plasmonic resonance of CND, and we confirmed it first by change the excitation and emission wavelength of CND. As shown in Fig. 6, only when both of excitation and emission wavelength of CND were close to those of CNC assembly (here was the CND4), the photoluminescent intensity could reach the maximum. This result was consistent with the phenomenon of plas monic resonance enhancement. Calculating the radiative recombination rate enhancement factor $(F)$ with the following equations:
$$
F={\frac{\left({\frac{1}{\tau^{*}}}\right)}{\left({\frac{1}{\tau}}\right)}}=\tau\int\tau^{*}
$$
$$
E R=(P L I_{\mathrm{CNC@ZIF-8@CNC}}/P L I_{\mathrm{CNC}})/P L I_{\mathrm{ZIF-8@CNC}}
$$
The calculated value of $F$ was 4.13, which was close to the actual value of the enhancement ratio (5.5 times from Eq. (10), Fig. 4b and 4c), indicating that the PL enhancement of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ could be ascribed to the coupling of CND and CNC. Meanwhile, the photoluminescent intensity of CND@CNC, in which CNC contacted CND directly, and without ZIF-8 was much lower than that of CNC. Such a photoluminescence quench indicated that the CND exerted a plasmonic effect on CNC, because plasmonic could enhanced photoluminescence only if there was an isolation layer, which was ZIF-8 in CND@ZIF${8@\mathrm{CNC}}$ .
$$
E Q Y^{*}{}_{m a x}=\frac{k_{\mathrm{AIE}}+C_{\mathrm{ext}}k_{\mathrm{sp}}}{k_{\mathrm{AIE}}+k_{\mathrm{nAIE}}+k_{\mathrm{sp}}}
$$
where the $k_{\mathrm{AIE}}$ is the recombination rate of aggregation-induced emis­ sion, $k_{\mathrm{{nAIE}}}$ is the recombination rate of non-aggregation-induced emis­ sion, and $k_{\mathrm{sp}}$ is the coupling rate of the exciton-located surface plasmon. The $C_{\mathrm{ext}}$ is the light extraction efficiency, which could be assumed to be $100\%$ (Okamoto et al., 2005). Since
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{E Q Y_{\mathrm{{CNC}}}=\frac{k_{\mathrm{AIE}}}{k_{\mathrm{AIE}}+k_{\mathrm{{nAIE}}}}}\\ &{\tau_{\mathrm{CNC}}=\frac{1}{k_{\mathrm{AIE}}+k_{\mathrm{{nAIE}}}}}\\ &{\tau^{*}=\frac{1}{k_{\mathrm{AIE}}+k_{\mathrm{{nAIE}}}+k_{\mathrm{{nAIE}}}}}\end{array}
$$
$$
E Q Y^{*}{}_{m a x}=\frac{\tau^{*}E Q Y_{\mathrm{CNC}}}{\tau_{\mathrm{CNC}}}\left[1+\frac{\tau_{\mathrm{CNC}}}{E Q Y_{\mathrm{CNC}}}\left(\frac{1}{\tau^{*}}-\frac{1}{\tau_{\mathrm{CNC}}}\right)\right]
$$
The attenuation of the light intensity through medium can be
described by Beer’s law：
$$
I\left(x\right)=I_{0}{\bf e}^{-a x}
$$
where $I(x)$ is intensity of light at distance of $x$ through the medium, $I_{0}$ is the original intensity of light, and $\alpha$ is attenuation coefficient of the medium.
Since the medium here were the solid ZIF-8 nanoparticles, the scat­ tering of the material should be considered, so the attenuation coeffi­ cient was the sum of absorption coefficient and scattering coefficient:
$$
a=k_{\mathrm{ab}}+k_{\mathrm{sc}}
$$
where $k_{\mathrm{ab}}$ is the absorption coefficient, and $k_{\mathrm{sc}}$ is the scattering coefficient.
To acquire the absorption coefficient and the scattering coefficient, ultraviolet spectrophotometry was used to test the material in the dispersion of water and at the solid state (Fig. S7). The absorption co­ efficient of the nanoparticles can be calculated via Lambert-Beer law.
$$
A=a b c
$$
where $A$ is absorbance of the nanoparticle in the dispersion, $b$ is the thickness of the solution layer, and c is the concentration of solution.
The calculation of scattering coefficient followed the Kubelka-Munk Theory:
$$
F(R\infty)\ =\ k\mathrm{ab}\Bigg/k\mathrm{sc}\ =\frac{(1-R_{\infty})^{2}}{2R_{\infty}}
$$
where $F(R_{\infty})$ is the Kubelka-Munk function, and $R_{\infty}$ is the absolute index of refraction. Through analyzing the data of ultraviolet spectropho­ tometry, the $k_{\mathrm{ab}}$ of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ was found to be $1.729\times10^{-3}$ $\mathbf{m}^{-1}$ , and $\displaystyle{k_{\mathrm{ab}}/k_{\mathrm{sc}}}$ was 0.022, so the $k_{\mathrm{sc}}$ was $3.241\times10^{-1}\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ . From the Eq. (15), $\alpha$ of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ was obtained to be $3.243\times10^{-1}\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ .
To study the distance of attenuation, we needed to know the actual height of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ during the emission quantum yield test. The height of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ could be described as:
$$
H_{\mathrm{n}}=H_{\mathrm{m}}\times{\frac{\rho_{b}}{\rho_{n}}}
$$
where $H_{\mathrm{n}}$ is the height of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ nanoparticles, $H_{\mathrm{m}}$ is the height of the test mold, which is $1.0\times10^{-3}\mathrm{m},\rho$ $\rho_{\mathsf{b}}$ is the bulk density of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ nanoparticles, and $\rho_{\mathrm{n}}$ is the actual density.
Since the content of CND was extremely low and could be ignored, the actual density of CND@ZIF-8@CNC can be explained as:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\uprho_{\mathrm{n}}=\upomega_{\mathrm{CNC}}\uprho_{\mathrm{c}}+\upomega_{\mathrm{ZIF}-8}\uprho_{\mathrm{z}}}\\ {\uprho_{\mathrm{CNC}}=1-\upomega_{\mathrm{ZIF}-8}}\end{array}
$$
where $\omega_{\mathrm{CNC}}$ is the mass ratio of CNC in ZIF- $8@\mathrm{CNC}$ , so the $\rho_{\mathrm{n}}$ was 1.535 $\times10^{6}\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ . $\rho_{\mathsf{b}}$ here was $4.290\times10^{5}{{\mathrm{g}}}{\cdot}{{\mathrm{m}}}^{-3}$ , and thus the $H_{\mathrm{n}}$ was 2.795 $\times10^{-4}\mathrm{m}$ .
Via putting the data into Eq. (16), the worst attenuation of CND@ZIF-8@CNC was $40.40\%$ . Because the attenuation of light is an exponential decay, we can use integral operation to get the attenuation value on average:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{A\mathrm{t}=\displaystyle\int_{0}^{H_{n}}e^{-\alpha x}d x}\\ {\displaystyle}\\ {A\mathrm{n}=\frac{A_{t}}{H_{n}}}\\ {E Q Y_{\mathrm{min}}=E Q Y_{\mathrm{CNC}}\times(E Q Y/E Q Y*)\times A_{\mathrm{n}}}\end{array}
$$
$$
E Q Y^{*}\mathrm{min}=E Q Y^{*}\mathrm{max}\times A_{\mathrm{n}}
$$
This result of $E Q Y_{\mathrm{min}}$ was $51.34\ \%$ , indicating that the EQY of $\mathrm{CND}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{CNC}$ should be in the range from $51.34\%-78.07\%$ , which was exactly consistent with the experimental data $(64.84\ \%)$ .
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we successfully established an assemble-induced emission composite with high emission quantum yield. In this compos­ ite, about 4 particles of cellulose nanocrystals (CNCs) were assembled closely in the nanoscale environment of metal organic frameworks (ZIF8) leading to a primary $50\ \%$ increase in emission intensity. Carbon nanodots (CNDs) were introduced into the ZIF- $\scriptstyle\cdot8@\mathrm{CNC}$ to remove the decrease in induced photo intensity from the extinction of ZIF-8, which also took the advantages of CND as a conductive nanoparticle with plasmonic enhancement. Such a plasmonic effect further increased the emission intensity by 5.5 times when the plasmonic wavelength was close to the diffraction wavelength of CNC assembly, and the final quantum yield of CNC reached to $64.84\ \%$ . Such a solid-state emission materials can be used to prepare invisible inks, and since they also maintained the high chemical modification ability and biocompatibility, they should also be of great potential in optical pesticide tracking and green catalysis.
